  {:slide/id :ftc
   :slide/title "Computing it: the fundamental theorem"
   :slide/blocks
   [{:block/id :ftc-text :block/kind :prose
     :md "Taking the limit of Riemann sums by hand is laborious. The fundamental theorem of calculus gives a shortcut: if F'(x) = f(x), then the integral of f from a to b is F(b) - F(a).\n\nFor f(x) = x^2 an antiderivative is F(x) = x^3 / 3, so the area is 1/3 - 0 = 1/3, which matches what the right sums (0.3984 at n = 8) were approaching from above."}
    {:block/id :ftc-tex :block/kind :tex
     :expr (= (integral (expt x 2) x 0 1) (- (/ (expt 1 3) 3) (/ (expt 0 3) 3)) (/ 1 3))}]}

  {:slide/id :shell
   :slide/title "Turning area into volume: the shell method"
   :slide/blocks
   [{:block/id :shell-intro :block/kind :prose
     :md "Now take the region under y = x^2 on [0, 2] and spin it about the y-axis. It sweeps out a solid. To find its volume, slice the region into thin vertical strips again, but spin each strip instead of measuring it: a strip at distance x from the axis, of height f(x) and width dx, becomes a thin cylindrical *shell* of radius x.\n\nUnrolled, a shell is nearly a flat slab of area 2 pi x times f(x) and thickness dx. Adding the shells up is again an integral:"}
    {:block/id :shell-tex :block/kind :tex
     :expr (= V (* 2 pi (integral (* x (expt x 2)) x 0 2)) (* 2 pi (/ (expt 2 4) 4)) (* 8 pi))}
    {:block/id :shell-fig :block/kind :figure
     :figure {:title "shell method: y = x^2 on [0, 2] turned about the y-axis"
              :view {:view :space :range [[-0.5 2.6] [-2.4 2.4] [-2.4 4.6]]
                     :camera {:target [1.0 0.0 0.2] :distance 12.0 :yaw -1.2 :pitch 0.42
                              :projection :perspective :zoom 1.6}}
              :params {:f :parabola :a 0.0 :b 2.0 :method :shell :n 6.0 :sweep 0.75}
              :objects [{:kind :revolution :id "solid" :color :blue
                         :f {:calc/param :f} :a {:calc/param :a} :b {:calc/param :b}
                         :method {:calc/param :method} :n {:calc/param :n}
                         :sweep {:calc/param :sweep}}]}}
    {:block/id :shell-png :block/kind :media :from :shell-fig :format :png}
    {:block/id :shell-note :block/kind :prose
     :md "The figure shows six shells, three quarters of the way round, so you can see inside. With more and thinner shells the stack fills the solid exactly, and the volume is 8 pi."}]}

  {:slide/id :recap
   :slide/title "Recap"
   :slide/blocks
   [{:block/id :recap-text :block/kind :prose
     :md "- The definite integral of f from a to b is the signed area under its graph.\n- It is defined as the limit of Riemann sums: thin rectangles whose total area converges.\n- The fundamental theorem computes it from an antiderivative: F(b) - F(a).\n- The same idea, summing thin pieces, gives volumes: spinning strips about the y-axis gives shells of volume 2 pi x f(x) dx.\n\nExercise: use shells to find the volume when y = x on [0, 1] is turned about the y-axis. (Answer: 2 pi / 3.)"}]}]}
